RustPythonCommand lineJavaScriptC <pre><code>use vexy_json::{parse_with_options, ParserOptions};

let strict = ParserOptions {
    allow_comments: false,
    allow_trailing_commas: false,
    allow_unquoted_keys: false,
    allow_single_quotes: false,
    implicit_top_level: false,
    newline_as_comma: false,
    ..ParserOptions::default()
};

assert!(parse_with_options("{a: 1}", strict.clone()).is_err());
assert!(parse_with_options(r#"{"a": [1, 2]}"#, strict).is_ok());
</code></pre> <pre><code>import vexy_json

vexy_json.parse_with_options('{"a": 1, }', allow_trailing_commas=True)
# {'a': 1}

vexy_json.parse_with_options('{"a": 1, }', allow_trailing_commas=False)
# ValueError: Parse error: Trailing comma at position 10
</code></pre> <p>Every option is a keyword argument with the default shown in the table. <code>vexy_json.load(fp, **options)</code> accepts the same keywords.</p> <pre><code>printf '{"a":1, }' | vexy-json --no-trailing-commas
</code></pre> <pre><code>Error:
&lt;stdin&gt;: Trailing comma at position 9
</code></pre> <p>The CLI has one <code>--no-…</code> flag for each syntax option and <code>--max-depth</code>. It always runs with <code>enable_repair</code> set to <code>false</code>; its own <code>--repair</code> flag works differently (see Command line).</p> <pre><code>import init, { parse_json_with_options } from "./pkg/vexy_json_wasm.js";
await init();

// input, allow_comments, allow_trailing_commas, allow_unquoted_keys,
// allow_single_quotes, implicit_top_level, newline_as_comma,
// enable_repair, max_depth (undefined means 128)
const json = parse_json_with_options(
  "{a: 1}", true, true, false, true, true, true, true, undefined,
);
// throws "Parse error: Expected string key but found UnquotedString at position 2"
</code></pre> <p>The WebAssembly function takes positional arguments. <code>max_repairs</code> is fixed at 100 and <code>fast_repair</code> and <code>report_repairs</code> at <code>false</code>.</p> <pre><code>VexyJsonParserOptions options = vexy_json_default_options();
options.allow_unquoted_keys = false;
VexyJsonParseResult result = vexy_json_parse_with_options("{a: 1}", &amp;options);
/* result.json is NULL, result.error holds the message */
vexy_json_free_result(result);
</code></pre>

Method Returns <code>is_null()</code>, <code>is_bool()</code>, <code>is_number()</code>, <code>is_string()</code>, <code>is_array()</code>, <code>is_object()</code> <code>bool</code> <code>as_bool()</code> <code>Option&lt;bool&gt;</code> <code>as_i64()</code> <code>Option&lt;i64&gt;</code> <code>as_f64()</code> <code>Option&lt;f64&gt;</code> <code>as_str()</code> <code>Option&lt;&amp;str&gt;</code> <code>as_array()</code> <code>Option&lt;&amp;Vec&lt;Value&gt;&gt;</code> <code>as_object()</code> <code>Option&lt;&amp;FxHashMap&lt;String, Value&gt;&gt;</code> <p><code>Value</code> implements <code>Index&lt;&amp;str&gt;</code> and <code>Index&lt;usize&gt;</code>. Indexing never panics: a missing key, an index out of range or indexing the wrong type returns <code>Value::Null</code>.</p> <pre><code>use vexy_json::{parse, Value};

let value = parse("{user: {name: 'Ada', roles: [admin, dev]}}").unwrap();

assert_eq!(value["user"]["name"].as_str(), Some("Ada"));
assert_eq!(value["user"]["roles"][1].as_str(), Some("dev"));
assert!(value["user"]["email"].is_null());

if let Value::Object(map) = &amp;value["user"] {
    println!("{} members", map.len()); // 2 members
}
</code></pre> <p><code>Number::as_f64()</code> converts either variant to <code>f64</code>.</p>
